{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# DC rate-limit headers are not honoured; a 429 is a hard failure\n\n## Context / Problem\n\nJira Data Center has built-in rate limiting (Administration → System → Rate limiting), introduced in\n**8.6**, **Data Center only**, and **OFF BY DEFAULT** — an administrator enables it. It is a per-user\nTOKEN BUCKET with a fill rate, not a fixed window.\n\nrebar's DC transport does not handle it at all. `_with_connection_retry` retries only\nconnection-level faults and its docstring states explicitly that `jira.exceptions.JIRAError` — \"any\nHTTP 4xx/5xx response\" — is NOT in the retry set. A 429 therefore propagates as a hard failure on the\nfirst occurrence, aborting whatever mutation or read was in flight, on an instance that was merely\nasking rebar to slow down.\n\nTwo constraints follow, and they shape the whole fix:\n\n1. **A static pacing policy is NOT implementable.** The limit is admin-configured and may be absent\n   entirely (pre-8.6, Server edition, or simply disabled), so rebar cannot hardcode a rate. The fix is\n   necessarily: honour the headers WHEN PRESENT, degrade gracefully when absent.\n2. **Atlassian prescribes a specific client behaviour** — sleep `Retry-After` seconds PLUS random\n   jitter of up to 20%. That differs from rebar's ADR-0036 backoff curve, which was designed for a\n   different failure shape (transient connectivity, where the server offers no guidance). Jitter here\n   is not decoration: every rebar instance hitting the same bucket would otherwise retry in lockstep\n   and re-collide.\n\n## Approach\n\n**Add a 429-specific retry path that honours `Retry-After` with up to 20% random jitter, kept separate\nfrom the connection-fault policy.**\n\nA 429 is a fundamentally different signal from a dropped connection: the server is healthy, it has\ntold us exactly how long to wait, and the wait is the remedy. Folding it into\n`_with_connection_retry`'s fixed 2s/5s curve would ignore the server's own instruction — the one piece\nof information that makes the retry correct — so the two policies stay distinct and the divergence\nfrom ADR-0036 is documented at the call site with this reason.\n\nConcretely, inside the DC retry wrapper `_with_connection_retry` (which WHERE IT GOES below\nmoves out of `transport.py`): catch `JIRAError`, and treat it as rate limiting\nONLY when the status is 429. Read `Retry-After` from the response headers; sleep that many seconds\nplus `random.uniform(0, 0.2) * retry_after`; retry. Everything else re-raises unchanged, preserving\ntoday's fail-fast behaviour for 4xx/5xx.\n\nThree bounds, because an unbounded honour-the-server loop is its own hazard:\n\n* **a retry cap** (3 attempts) — a persistently rate-limited instance must fail loudly rather than\n  sleep indefinitely inside a pass;\n* **a per-wait ceiling** (60s) — a misconfigured or hostile `Retry-After: 3600` must not hang a\n  reconcile pass for an hour. Exceeding it fails with the value named, so the operator sees why;\n* **a missing or unparseable `Retry-After`** falls back to the existing connection-retry backoff\n  rather than guessing — a 429 without the header is out-of-contract, and inventing a delay would be\n  the \"plausible mechanism\" mistake this epic keeps recording.\n\n**Absence of rate-limit headers is NORMAL, not an error.** On a pre-8.6, Server-edition, or\nrate-limiting-disabled instance no 429 ever arrives and this path never executes. Nothing may warn,\nlog, or degrade on their absence — the default deployment must be completely unaffected.\n\n**Rejected: proactive pacing from the `X-RateLimit-*` headers on successful responses.** It sounds\nbetter (avoid the 429 rather than react to it) but it requires a rate to pace against, which is\nexactly what is not knowable when the feature may be disabled. It would also add per-request\nbookkeeping to every call for a feature most instances do not have enabled.\n\n**Rejected: reusing ADR-0036's backoff curve.** It discards `Retry-After`, which is the server telling\nus the answer.\n\n**WHERE IT GOES — the module-size cap forces the shape, so the plan states it up front.**\n`adapters/jira_datacenter/transport.py` is at **789 LOC** against a LOCKED 800-line cap with no\nallowlist, so a ~50-line 429 path cannot land in it. Rather than discover that at implementation\ntime (bug 21fc's plan named a site the cap then forced it out of, and its close failed on the stale\nplan), this plan names the placement now:\n\nExtract the retry/error-translation cluster from `transport.py` into a sibling\n`adapters/jira_datacenter/retry.py`, and add the 429 policy there. The cluster already exists as a\ncall-graph seam — `_connection_retry_exceptions`, `_jira_http_error_types`, `_as_backend_http_error`,\n`_tls_verification_error` and `_with_connection_retry` call each other, and the call graph was\nCHECKED rather than assumed: the four internals have exactly ONE caller each (each other), while\n`_with_connection_retry` is the only widely-used member at 15 call sites — which is precisely why\nimporting it back under its existing name leaves every one of them untouched. That is exactly the\nsanctioned split (AGENTS.md: extract a cluster of\nfunctions that already call each other, never split mechanically to hit a number). It also clears the\nsub-100-LOC floor, since the moved cluster is ~130 lines before the new policy is added at all.\n\nThe move is BEHAVIOUR-PRESERVING and asserted so: the extracted functions move verbatim, and\n`transport.py` imports them under their existing names, so every call site is unchanged. That\nseparation is a REASON, not just a size remedy — a 429 is the server telling us the delay, and the\nconnection-fault policy is for when it cannot; keeping the two policies in one file is what would\ninvite folding them together.\n\n**A pycontribs detail to verify at RUNTIME, not from docs:** how `JIRAError` exposes the status and\nthe response headers (`.status_code`, `.response.headers`). The library's surface is split and\n`@translate_resource_args` defeats static lookup, so the implementation must confirm the attribute\nshape against the installed version before relying on it, and the tests must construct the error the\nsame way the library does.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/adapters/jira_datacenter/retry.py` — NEW: the retry/error\n  cluster extracted verbatim from `transport.py`, plus the 429 policy.\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/adapters/jira_datacenter/transport.py` — imports the cluster\n  under its existing names; no call site changes.\n- `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/` — held-out regression tests (new module).\n\n## Testing\n\n* **headers present** — a client raising 429 with `Retry-After: 2`, then succeeding: assert the call\n  succeeds, that a sleep of at least 2s and at most 2.4s was requested (the 20% jitter band), and that\n  the result is the real one. Sleep is injected/patched so the test does not actually wait.\n* **jitter is applied and bounded** — over repeated runs the delay varies and always lands within\n  `[retry_after, retry_after * 1.2]`. Asserting only \"sleep was called\" would pass a lockstep\n  implementation, which is the thing jitter exists to prevent.\n* **headers absent** — no 429 ever raised: assert the rate-limit path is never entered and behaviour\n  is byte-for-byte today's. This is the DEFAULT deployment and must be untouched.\n* **429 without `Retry-After`** — falls back to the connection-retry backoff rather than inventing a\n  delay.\n* **the retry cap** — a client that 429s forever fails after the cap instead of looping.\n* **the per-wait ceiling** — `Retry-After: 3600` fails with the value named rather than sleeping.\n* **other JIRAErrors are unaffected** — a 400 and a 500 still raise immediately, asserted, since this\n  change touches the shared error path for every DC call.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n\n- [ ] a 429 carrying `Retry-After` is retried after sleeping that many seconds plus up to 20% jitter,\n      asserted with an injected sleep so the delay is observable without waiting\n- [ ] the jitter is real and bounded: observed delays vary across runs and always fall within\n      `[retry_after, retry_after * 1.2]`\n- [ ] absence of rate-limit headers is handled as NORMAL — no warning, no degradation, no behaviour\n      change — asserted by a test proving the rate-limit path is never entered\n- [ ] a 429 WITHOUT a usable `Retry-After` falls back to the existing backoff instead of a guessed\n      delay\n- [ ] a persistently rate-limited instance fails after a bounded number of attempts rather than\n      sleeping indefinitely, asserted\n- [ ] an absurd `Retry-After` (3600) is refused with the value named rather than hanging the pass\n- [ ] non-429 `JIRAError`s (400, 500) still raise immediately — asserted, because this touches the\n      shared error path for every DC call\n- [ ] the divergence from ADR-0036's backoff curve is documented at the call site WITH the reason\n      (the server supplies the delay; jitter prevents lockstep re-collision)\n- [ ] the retry cluster's extraction is behaviour-preserving: `transport.py` keeps every call site\n      unchanged, and both it and the new `retry.py` are within the 800-LOC cap, verified per file"}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785533440819345001,"uuid":"2b79b427-83ae-46c6-911a-e700a2cc0649"}